            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>Running ads for an independent store, the biggest fear isn't spending money—it's spending it with no results. Facebook, Google, TikTok, Pinterest... every platform tempts you to try, but the budget gets fragmented, each campaign shuts down after three days, and you never build meaningful data.</p>
<p>This article provides a practical budget allocation checklist, broken down by phase and channel, to help you maximize ROI with limited ad spend.</p>
<h2>Before Allocating: Answer These 3 Questions First</h2>
<p>Before opening any ad dashboard, complete these checks:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Define your core objective</strong>: Pursuing immediate ROI or building scale? New store owners should prioritize ROI</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Set minimum test budget per channel</strong>: At least $300/week per ad set, or data won't be statistically meaningful</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Establish total budget ceiling</strong>: Monthly ad spend shouldn't exceed 30% of liquid capital</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: If your monthly ad budget is under $5,000, focus on 1-2 channels first. Don't spread yourself thin.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Two Different Allocation Logic: Test Phase vs. Stabilization Phase</h2>
<h3>Test Phase (First 4 Weeks): Small Steps, Fast Iteration</h3>
<p>The core goal isn't making money—it's finding audiences and creatives that can make money.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Phase</th>
<th>Budget %</th>
<th>Key Actions</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Primary channel</td>
<td>60-70%</td>
<td>Deep test on one main platform</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Secondary channels</td>
<td>20-30%</td>
<td>Maintain presence, gather baseline data</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Experimental</td>
<td>10%</td>
<td>Test new formats, new audiences</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>:<br />
- [ ] Test at least 3 different audience targeting per channel<br />
- [ ] Run at least 1 creative version per ad placement<br />
- [ ] Log CPA and add-to-cart rate for each campaign<br />
- [ ] Week 2 review: Kill ad sets with CPA above 2x target</p>
<h3>Stabilization Phase (Week 5+): Scale What's Working, Cut What's Not</h3>
<p>Once you find 2-3 positive ROI ad sets, it's time to scale.</p>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>:<br />
- [ ] Concentrate 70% of budget on top 20% performing ad sets<br />
- [ ] Monitor CPA daily; pause immediately if threshold exceeded<br />
- [ ] Scale by no more than 20% weekly to avoid model instability<br />
- [ ] Set automation rules: pause when CPA exceeds 1.5x target</p>
<h2>Channel Budget Allocation Reference</h2>
<h3>Facebook/Meta (Best for B2C e-commerce, trending products)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Recommended allocation: 40-50% of total ad budget</li>
<li>Priority: Dynamic Product Ads + retargeting audiences</li>
<li>Pitfall to avoid: Don't start with Broad targeting—new stores lack sufficient data</li>
</ul>
<h3>Google Ads (Best for products with search demand)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Recommended allocation: 20-30% of total ad budget</li>
<li>Priority: Shopping ads &gt; Search ads &gt; P-Max</li>
<li>Cold start tip: Run Manual CPC for 2 weeks to gather conversion data before switching to Smart Bidding</li>
</ul>
<h3>TikTok (Best for Gen Z consumer products)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Recommended allocation: 15-25% of total ad budget</li>
<li>Priority: Video Shopping Ads + TikTok Sparks</li>
<li>Note: Creative lifespan is short—refresh with 3-5 new videos weekly</li>
</ul>
<h3>Retargeting Allocation</h3>
<p>Regardless of channel split, <strong>retargeting should receive at least 20% of total budget</strong>. Users who've already visited your store convert at 1/3 to 1/5 the cost of new users—they're your most valuable traffic.</p>
<h2>5 Metrics to Check Daily/Weekly</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Daily CPA</strong>: Is it within target range?</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Ad frequency</strong>: Is the percentage of users with frequency over 5 too high? (Too high means audience is too narrow)</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Add-to-cart rate vs. conversion rate</strong>: High cart adds but low conversions indicate landing page or checkout issues, not just ad problems</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Ad fatigue</strong>: Replace creatives immediately when same creative's CTR drops by 30%</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Budget burn rate</strong>: If budget depletes by afternoon, either bids are too low or competition increased—adjust accordingly</li>
</ul>
<h2>Common Budget Allocation Mistakes</h2>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Mistake 1: Equal budget split across all channels</strong><br />
Correct approach: Dynamically adjust based on each channel's ROI; concentrate resources on highest returns</p>
<p><strong>Mistake 2: Expecting positive ROI during test phase</strong><br />
Correct approach: Test phase allows controlled losses; goal is finding a scalable model</p>
<p><strong>Mistake 3: Adjusting budget too frequently</strong><br />
Correct approach: Observe changes for 3-5 days minimum before deciding to revert</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Quick Execution Checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] List all currently running ad channels</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm test budget and target CPA for each channel</li>
<li>[ ] Open ad dashboard, flag ad sets with CPA above 2x target</li>
<li>[ ] Pause the bottom 20% performing ad sets</li>
<li>[ ] Reallocate freed budget to top 2 ad sets</li>
<li>[ ] Set daily monitoring reminders for this week</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Budget allocation isn't a one-time decision—it's an ongoing optimization process.</strong> Execute this checklist for 2 weeks and you'll see noticeable improvement in data quality.</p>
